\title{A Fixed-Point Bridge: Self-Modifying Halting and\\ Lawvere--Cantor Diagonalization}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous bridge between two areas usually treated separately: the
fixed-point/diagonal theory of set theory (Lawvere's fixed-point theorem and, as
its contrapositive, Cantor's theorem) and the computability of \emph{self-modifying}
programs---machines that may rewrite their own code during execution. We introduce
a formal model of self-modifying machines and establish three results. First, a
\textbf{Simulation Theorem}: every self-modifying machine is behaviorally
equivalent to a fixed-program machine obtained by encoding the program as data, so
self-modification adds no computational power. Second, a \textbf{genuine,
non-vacuous undecidability theorem}: an explicit self-modifying machine whose
halting predicate coincides with the classical halting problem of a universal
model of computation, hence is not computable; combined with the Simulation
Theorem, self-modifying halting is exactly as hard as classical halting---no
harder, no easier. Third, a \textbf{Behavioral Fixed-Point Theorem} (Kleene's
recursion theorem, read operationally): no computable rewriting rule can change the
behavior of every program. We stress a methodological point: a naïve
diagonalization statement quantifying over a ``complete enumeration of
predicates'' is vacuously true by Cantor's theorem; our undecidability result
avoids this by reducing to the honest halting problem. Throughout, Cantor, Turing,
and Kleene appear as three readings of a single diagonal fixed-point principle.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Self-modifying code---a program that rewrites its own instructions while it runs---appears
throughout computing, from metamorphic malware and just-in-time compilers to
speculative self-improving software. A recurring intuition is that such systems are
qualitatively more powerful, or at least harder to analyze, than fixed-program
computation. This paper makes that intuition precise and largely dissolves it.

Our organizing principle is the \emph{diagonal fixed-point argument}, isolated in
cleanest form by Lawvere. A function $f$ has a \emph{fixed point} if $f(b)=b$ for
some $b$. Lawvere's theorem says a sufficiently rich indexing of functions forces
every self-map to have a fixed point; its contrapositive, using a fixed-point-free
map, is Cantor's theorem. We show the halting problem for self-modifying machines,
and Kleene's recursion theorem, are further readings of the same principle.

We prove three main theorems: a Simulation Theorem (Section~\ref{sec:sim}); a
non-vacuous undecidability theorem (Section~\ref{sec:undec}); and a behavioral
fixed-point theorem (Section~\ref{sec:kleene}). Section~\ref{sec:bridge} packages
the set-theoretic and computational impossibilities into a single statement.

\section{Preliminaries}

For $g : A \to (A \to B)$ we say $g$ is \emph{point-surjective} if it is surjective
into the function space $A \to B$. Let $\Bool = \{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}$
and $\lnot : \Bool \to \Bool$ be Boolean negation.

For computability we fix a standard universal model of partial recursive
functions. Programs are codes in a countable set $\Codes$; each $c$ denotes a
partial function $\varphi_c : \mathbb{N} \rightharpoonup \mathbb{N}$, and we write
$\varphi_c(n)\down$ when the computation of $c$ on input $n$ converges. A bounded
universal evaluator $E(s,c,n)$ simulates $c$ on $n$ for a budget of $s$ steps,
returning either an output or the symbol $\bot$ (``no result yet''). It is
\emph{sound} (any output is a true output of $c$ on $n$) and \emph{complete} (if
$\varphi_c(n)$ converges to $x$, some budget $k$ gives $E(k,c,n)=x$), so
\[ \varphi_c(n)\down \iff \exists k,\; E(k,c,n) \neq \bot. \]
The predicate $c \mapsto \varphi_c(n)\down$ is not computable for the appropriate
universal input $n$; this classical halting problem is our bedrock undecidable set.

\section{The diagonal fixed-point principle}

\begin{theorem}[Lawvere, point-surjective form]\label{thm:lawvere}
Let $A,B$ be types and $g : A \to (A \to B)$ be point-surjective. Then every
self-map $f : B \to B$ has a fixed point: there exists $b$ with $f(b)=b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Consider $d : A \to B$, $d(a) = f\big(g(a)(a)\big)$. By point-surjectivity there is
$a_\star$ with $g(a_\star)=d$. Set $b = g(a_\star)(a_\star)$. Then
\[ f(b) = f\big(g(a_\star)(a_\star)\big) = d(a_\star) = g(a_\star)(a_\star) = b, \]
using the definition of $d$ and $g(a_\star)=d$. Hence $f(b)=b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No point-surjection via a fixed-point-free map]\label{thm:nops}
If $f : B \to B$ has no fixed point, there is no point-surjective $g : A \to (A \to B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:lawvere}: such $g$ would force a fixed point of $f$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cantor, Boolean form]\label{cor:cantorbool}
For any type $A$, there is no surjection $g : A \to (A \to \Bool)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:nops} with $f=\lnot$, which is fixed-point-free.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cantor, power-set form]
For any $A$ and any $g : A \to \mathcal{P}(A)$, $g$ is not surjective.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The diagonal subset $\{a : a \notin g(a)\}$ is not in the image of $g$; equivalently
apply Theorem~\ref{thm:nops} to negation.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No universal decider for a complete enumeration]\label{thm:diageng}
Let $\mathrm{enum} : \alpha \to (\alpha \to \Bool)$ be point-surjective. Then there
is no total table $d : \alpha \to (\alpha \to \Bool)$ with $d(i)(a) =
\mathrm{enum}(i)(a)$ for all $i,a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The existence of a point-surjective $\mathrm{enum}$ contradicts
Corollary~\ref{cor:cantorbool}; the hypothesis is unsatisfiable and the conclusion
holds a fortiori.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Vacuity warning]\label{rem:vacuity}
Theorem~\ref{thm:diageng} is true but, as a statement about real machines,
\emph{vacuous}: its hypothesis can never hold, by Corollary~\ref{cor:cantorbool}.
Any ``undecidability theorem'' of the form ``if there were a complete enumeration
then no decider exists'' proves nothing about actual systems. A substantive result
must exhibit a concrete machine and reduce a known-nonempty undecidable problem to
it. This drives Section~\ref{sec:undec}.
\end{remark}

\section{Self-modifying machines and the Simulation Theorem}\label{sec:sim}

\begin{definition}[Self-modifying machine]
For types $P$ (programs), $S$ (states), a \emph{self-modifying machine} is a step
$\stepf : P \to S \to (P \times S)_\bot$, whose value is $(p',s')$ (continue with
possibly new program $p'$) or the halt symbol $\bot$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Run and halting]
The $n$-step run from a configuration $q \in P \times S$ is
\[ \run(q,0)=q, \quad
\run(q,n{+}1)=\begin{cases}\bot&\text{if }\stepf(q)=\bot,\\ \run(q',n)&\text{if }\stepf(q)=q'.\end{cases}\]
The machine \emph{halts} from $q$ if $\run(q,n)=\bot$ for some $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Standard machine and simulation]
A \emph{standard machine} over $S$ has step $\stepf : S \to S_\bot$; only the state
evolves. Given a self-modifying $M$ over $(P,S)$, its \emph{standard simulation}
$\widehat{M}$ is the standard machine over $P \times S$ with
$\widehat{\stepf}(q) = \bot$ if $M.\stepf(q)=\bot$, and $\widehat{\stepf}(q)=q'$ if
$M.\stepf(q)=q'$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Runs coincide]\label{lem:runs}
For every self-modifying $M$, configuration $q$, and $n \in \mathbb{N}$,
$M.\run(q,n) = \widehat{M}.\run(q,n)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. For $n=0$ both are $q$. For the step,
$\widehat{M}.\stepf(q) = M.\stepf(q)$ by construction; in the $\bot$ case both
return $\bot$, in the successor case both reduce to the length-$n$ run from $q'$,
equal by hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Simulation Theorem]\label{thm:sim}
$M$ halts from $q$ $\iff$ $\widehat{M}$ halts from $q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both sides are $\exists n,\ \run(q,n)=\bot$ for the respective machines; by
Lemma~\ref{lem:runs} the runs agree for every $n$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:sim} is the formal expression of ``code is data'': rewriting one's
program confers no behavioral power beyond a fixed program on an encoding.

\section{A genuine undecidable self-modifying machine}\label{sec:undec}

\begin{definition}[Diagonal machine]
Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$. Define $D_n$ over programs $\Codes$ and states $\mathbb{N}$
(a budget) by
\[ \stepf(c,s)=\begin{cases}\bot&\text{if }E(s,c,n)\neq\bot,\\ (c,\,s{+}1)&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Run characterization]\label{lem:runchar}
For all $n,c,s,N$,
\[ D_n.\run\big((c,s),N\big)=\bot \iff \exists i<N,\ E(s{+}i,c,n)\neq\bot. \]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $N$. For $N=0$ both sides are false. For $N{+}1$: if $E(s,c,n)\neq\bot$,
the step halts and $i=0$ works; otherwise the run reduces to the length-$N$ run from
budget $s{+}1$, giving by hypothesis $\exists i<N,\ E((s{+}1){+}i,c,n)\neq\bot$, i.e.
after re-indexing $\exists i<N{+}1,\ E(s{+}i,c,n)\neq\bot$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bridge Lemma]\label{thm:bridge}
For all $n,c$, $\ D_n$ halts from $(c,0)$ $\iff \varphi_c(n)\down$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:runchar} with $s=0$, the machine halts iff some budget $i$ makes
$E(i,c,n)\neq\bot$. By soundness such an output witnesses convergence; by
completeness convergence yields such a budget. Hence halting $\iff \varphi_c(n)\down$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Undecidability of self-modifying halting]\label{thm:undec}
For every $n$, the predicate $c \mapsto \big(D_n$ halts from $(c,0)\big)$ is not
computable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge} it equals $c \mapsto \varphi_c(n)\down$, the classical
halting problem, which is not computable.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Undecidability persists under simulation]\label{thm:undecstd}
For every $n$, $c \mapsto \big(\widehat{D_n}$ halts from $(c,0)\big)$ is not
computable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sim} this predicate coincides with that of
Theorem~\ref{thm:undec}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact difficulty]
Self-modifying halting is neither harder nor easier than classical halting: the
upper bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:sim}, the lower bound Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:undec} is non-vacuous precisely because it reduces to a
known-nonempty undecidable set, avoiding the trap of Remark~\ref{rem:vacuity}.
\end{remark}

\subsection*{Worked micro-examples}
Consider three small programs. (i) If $\varphi_{c_0}(n)=0$ converges after $t$
internal steps, then $E(s,c_0,n)=\bot$ for $s<t$ and $\neq\bot$ for $s\ge t$, so
$D_n$ increments the budget $t$ times and halts---matching $\varphi_{c_0}(n)\down$.
(ii) If $c_1$ converges exactly on even inputs, $D_n$ from $(c_1,0)$ halts iff $n$
is even. (iii) If $\varphi_{c_2}(n)$ diverges, $E(s,c_2,n)=\bot$ for all $s$ and
$D_n$ increments the budget forever. Thus $D_n$ is a faithful one-sided
semi-decision procedure; Theorem~\ref{thm:undec} is the impossibility of turning
it into a two-sided decider.

\section{Kleene's recursion theorem: behavioral fixed points}\label{sec:kleene}

\begin{theorem}[Behavioral Fixed-Point / Kleene recursion]\label{thm:kleene}
Let $\mathrm{modify} : \Codes \to \Codes$ be computable. Then there exists $c$ with
$\varphi_{\mathrm{modify}(c)} = \varphi_c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Kleene's recursion theorem for the universal model: every computable transformation
on programs has a program whose behavior it fixes, built via the $s$--$m$--$n$
theorem from a program that computes its own code.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The identity rewrite has a behavioral fixed point (every $c$), so the hypothesis of
Theorem~\ref{thm:kleene} is satisfiable.
\end{corollary}

No computable self-modification rule can change the behavior of every program: some
program is a behavioral quine, reproducing its input--output function no matter how
its text is rewritten---the diagonal principle in the category of computable maps.

\section{The bridge, in one statement}\label{sec:bridge}

\begin{theorem}[Cross-domain bridge]
Fix a universal input $n$. Over the configuration space $\Codes$ the following hold
simultaneously, all instances of the diagonal fixed-point principle:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Lawvere--Cantor) there is no surjection $g : \Codes \to (\Codes \to \Bool)$;
\item (Computability) $c \mapsto \big(D_n$ halts from $(c,0)\big)$ is not computable;
\item (Standard form) $c \mapsto \big(\widehat{D_n}$ halts from $(c,0)\big)$ is not
computable.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Part 1 is Corollary~\ref{cor:cantorbool}; part 2 is Theorem~\ref{thm:undec}; part 3
is Theorem~\ref{thm:undecstd}.
\end{proof}

The first is Cantor, the second and third are Turing, and the shared engine is
Lawvere's fixed-point theorem.

\section{Applications and future directions}

\textbf{Applications.} Metamorphic malware cannot be exhaustively classified by any
general halting analyzer, but by Theorem~\ref{thm:sim} treating mutated code as
data reduces the problem to the (no harder) classical case. Any computable
self-improvement rule has programs it cannot behaviorally alter
(Theorem~\ref{thm:kleene}), limiting guaranteed improvement schemes. The bridge
also gives a unified pedagogical route to Cantor, halting, and Kleene.

\textbf{Future directions.} (1) Build a machine that provably rewrites its program
infinitely often yet realizes the halting problem, quantifying
``self-modification depth'' and relating it to the arithmetical hierarchy. (2)
Prove the self-modifying halting set is $\Sigma^0_1$-complete (many-one equivalent
to $\emptyset'$). (3) Strengthen Theorem~\ref{thm:kleene} to a uniform fixed point
computable from the rule, connecting to explicit quines. (4) Re-derive halting
undecidability categorically as Lawvere's theorem in the category of computable
maps.

\section{Conclusion}

We built a bridge from Lawvere's fixed-point theorem to the halting problem for
self-modifying code. Self-modification adds no power; a concrete diagonal machine
yields a genuine undecidability result reducing to classical halting; and Kleene's
recursion theorem guarantees behavioral fixed points against any rewriting rule.
Cantor, Turing, and Kleene are three readings of one diagonal fixed-point
principle.

